{
  "content": "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Rebuild the governing task spec from all user turns, in order**\n- Treat the user conversation as a running constraint ledger, not a snapshot from the latest turn.\n- Each new user message can add, narrow, override, or resolve earlier ambiguity; re-evaluate the whole specification after every decisive update.\n- Use only user-stated facts, relations, quantities, and output requirements. Do not import assistant guesses, \u201clikely\u201d continuations, or stale assumptions.\n- If the later user trajectory fully determines the task, earlier uncertainty should be treated as resolved rather than still live.\n\n**2) Judge the assistant against the latest complete user state**\n- Evaluate correctness relative to the fully merged user specification, not the partial subproblem that existed earlier.\n- If the assistant answered an earlier version correctly but missed a later constraint, mark the response stale rather than partially sufficient.\n- Repeated clarification-seeking becomes a divergence only when the cumulative user turns already contain enough information to proceed.\n- Prefer context replacement over patching old reasoning: once the user updates the task, the analyzer should compare against the updated end-state.\n\n**3) Separate local correctness from global correctness**\n- A response can be valid within a sub-derivation and still fail the conversation because it stops before the full asked-for outcome.\n- Preserve correct intermediate work, but do not let it mask an incomplete final answer or a shift to a smaller subquestion.\n- When later user facts change the target, explicitly distinguish \u201cright for the old problem\u201d from \u201cright for the resolved problem.\u201d\n- If the assistant carries through a valid calculation but on the wrong cumulative spec, flag the missing update rather than the arithmetic.\n\n**4) Treat later user facts as constraint updates, not side notes**\n- Late-disclosed details often change the target quantity, reference set, or scope of aggregation.\n- Look for additions that alter what must be covered, what remains, what is included, or how a total should be interpreted.\n- Separate user-given facts from derived values so the analyzer can see which numbers are fixed and which must be recomputed.\n- When a later shard introduces a new terminal quantity or changed endpoint, earlier \u201cfinal\u201d values may have become intermediate states.\n\n**5) Detect stale anchoring and fragmented context**\n- A common failure mode is optimizing around an old ambiguity after the user has resolved it.\n- Watch for assistants that treat each shard as standalone instead of merging shards into one coherent model.\n- When the user\u2019s later wording replaces an earlier assumption, discard the obsolete branch; do not keep both interpretations alive.\n- The key signal is persistent reasoning on an outdated frame even after the conversation has made that frame unnecessary.\n\n**6) Flag unsupported semantic shifts and hidden assumptions**\n- If the assistant turns an under-specified value into a specific inferred one without user support, treat that as an unsupported leap.\n- Do not bless invented continuations, default rules, or semantic rewrites just because earlier fragments made them seem plausible.\n- When the user later defines a term or relationship, that explicit framing should override earlier assumptions.\n- The first unsupported leap often causes a cascade; identify it early rather than only the downstream inconsistency.\n\n**7) Recompute totals, residuals, and aggregates when scope changes**\n- Late facts may change the denominator, remainder pool, or set of items being counted; check whether each user-provided component is included exactly once and at the right scope.\n- Look for \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cof the rest,\u201d \u201clast,\u201d or similar language that signals a different base than the original total.\n- Verify both undercounting and overcounting when the user has introduced multiple entities, stages, or contributions across turns.\n- If a later update changes the target total, prior exact arithmetic may still be stale because it used the old scope.\n\n**8) Separate whole-vs-subset reasoning carefully**\n- Distinguish quantities tied to the whole from quantities tied to a narrowed subset or later stage.\n- After each update, confirm the current reference set before judging the next derived quantity.\n- A frequent divergence is carrying forward an old denominator, pool, or baseline after the user has already narrowed it.\n- Rebuild the current state at each turn so later deductions use the correct reference frame.\n\n**9) Use a state timeline when multiple turns describe one evolving quantity**\n- Track how the relevant quantity changes across turns instead of solving from isolated fragments.\n- If later statements refer to the same actor, object, or amount, combine them before evaluating the assistant.\n- Distinguish between facts that define the same variable through successive transformations and facts that introduce a new final quantity that must be folded back in.\n- This helps spot cases where the assistant treated an intermediate state as the endpoint.\n\n**10) Make corrective notes concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The issue description should name the missed action: incorporate the latest user fact, stop asking for already provided information, carry the subtotal to the final answer, or drop an unsupported branch.\n- Avoid vague advice like \u201cbe more careful\u201d; specify what the assistant failed to integrate or finalize.\n- If the main failure is stale control flow rather than arithmetic, say so directly.\n- Keep the corrective direction grounded in the user\u2019s messages, not in a proposed implementation method.\n\n**11) Preserve aligned subcomputations while isolating the divergence**\n- When the assistant\u2019s early steps are sound but the final conclusion is wrong, keep the aligned work and identify the exact point where the spec stopped matching.\n- This prevents over-penalizing valid derivations while still flagging the incomplete or stale end result.\n- In multi-step tasks, the analyzer should be able to say \u201cthis part is correct, but it is now being applied to the wrong cumulative problem.\u201d\n- That distinction is especially important when later user facts invalidate only the target, not the earlier arithmetic.\n\n**12) Prefer merged-trajectory evaluation over shard-by-shard judgment**\n- The strongest strategy is to reconstruct the implied whole from the full user trajectory, then compare each assistant response against that unified view.\n- Preserve verified facts and correct deductions, but remove speculative scaffolding that could bias later judgments.\n- This approach catches when the assistant answers the latest fragment in isolation instead of the accumulated conversation state.\n- In S2-style repairs, compact to the cleaned cumulative spec plus the validated reasoning, and drop any conclusion that depends on superseded assumptions.",
  "version": 3,
  "history": [
    "",
    "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Build and maintain a live task spec from user turns only**\n- Reconstruct the intended task incrementally from *all* user messages so far, not from the latest one in isolation.\n- Treat each new user message as a constraint update that can refine, complete, or override earlier uncertainty.\n- Re-evaluate prior judgments whenever new user facts arrive; earlier \u201cmissing information\u201d may become obsolete once later shards fill the gap.\n- Keep the spec canonical and user-derived: include only stated quantities, relationships, and output requirements, not inferred extras or assistant-proposed branches.\n\n**2) Judge against the resolved end-state, not intermediate uncertainty**\n- Separate what was merely reasonable given partial information from what remains correct after the full conversation is considered.\n- If later user messages complete the needed information, reassess earlier clarification requests or partial answers against the updated full spec.\n- When a decisive fact appears, it should override earlier branches rather than sit beside them as a competing interpretation.\n- A response can be locally correct yet globally stale; flag it if new user constraints invalidate the earlier conclusion.\n\n**3) Detect when later user turns change the arithmetic or structure**\n- Look for late-disclosed quantities or modifiers that change the model, not just add detail.\n- Treat phrases that introduce repetition, grouping, or per-entity application as possible structure changes that require recomputing aggregates.\n- Compare each new user message against the assistant\u2019s most recent numeric claim to see whether the arithmetic basis has shifted.\n- Mark earlier exact answers as superseded if a later turn adds a dimension that changes the final total or target value.\n\n**4) Distinguish partial progress from full closure**\n- Supportive intermediate derivations may be correct, but do not let them mask that the requested end result still hasn\u2019t been reached.\n- If the assistant stops at a subtotal, invariant, or partial relation while the conversation has already expanded to the full problem, classify that as incomplete.\n- Separate derived facts from asked-for outputs: verify that the assistant actually connected the full constraint set to the final requested quantity or format.\n- Prefer correction over endorsement when an answer is right for a subproblem but not for the final accumulated task.\n\n**5) Recompute when the current user state is sufficient**\n- \u201cIncomplete\u201d should be judged only against the currently accumulated spec, not against hypothetical future details.\n- If the user has already provided enough information to solve the task, the analyzer should expect integration and completion rather than another request for more data.\n- Repeatedly asking for information that has already been supplied shard-by-shard is a strong divergence signal.\n- The final user turn should trigger a fresh evaluation of the whole expression or reasoning chain, not a continuation of earlier uncertainty.\n\n**6) Diagnose stale uncertainty and dead-end branching**\n- When the assistant keeps optimizing around an earlier ambiguity after the user has resolved it, flag that as anchoring on obsolete uncertainty.\n- If the conversation has settled on one intended interpretation, do not preserve discarded alternatives in the analysis.\n- Use compacted context to strip away obsolete branches, provisional assumptions, and uncertainty-driven scaffolding so evaluation starts from the resolved state.\n- Prefer a single clean conclusion once the intended answer is evident; reopening settled branches is usually noise, not rigor.\n\n**7) Verify output requirements as part of alignment**\n- Check not only whether the content is right, but whether the response matches the user\u2019s explicit output constraint.\n- A bare result, specific format, or constrained presentation should be enforced even when the reasoning is otherwise sound.\n- Side commentary, alternate formats, or extra explanation can be a mismatch if the user asked for a minimal output.\n\n**8) Make \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The correction should identify the actual missed action: recompute, incorporate a later constraint, stop asking for already-provided info, or finish the final step.\n- Avoid vague commentary about being careful or reconsidering; state the specific divergence from the user\u2019s accumulated intent.\n- If the main failure is that the assistant never updated the answer after new user information, that should be named directly.\n\n**9) Use symbolic reconstruction to expose mismatches**\n- Before judging, translate the user\u2019s stated relationships into a clean internal model of variables, constraints, and dependencies.\n- This makes it easier to see whether the assistant stopped at an intermediate invariant, used an outdated branch, or failed to propagate a newly supplied fact through the whole chain.\n- Keep all user-stated quantities and relationships together in one coherent view so omissions and double-counting become visible.\n\n**10) Watch for common aggregate errors across turns**\n- A robust analysis should catch both undercounting and overcounting when multiple user-supplied components accumulate over time.\n- Check that each user-provided component is included exactly once in the final interpretation.\n- When the task evolves by adding terms or factors, verify the assistant recomputes the aggregate rather than freezing an earlier subtotal.",
    "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Reconstruct the task spec cumulatively from user turns only**\n- Build the canonical task spec from all user messages so far, not from the latest turn or the assistant\u2019s inferred pattern.\n- Treat each new user shard as a constraint update: it may add, narrow, override, or resolve earlier ambiguity.\n- Keep only user-stated facts, relationships, quantities, and output requirements; do not import assistant assumptions or \u201clikely\u201d continuations.\n- If later user text makes the problem fully determined, stop treating earlier uncertainty as live.\n\n**2) Re-evaluate after every decisive user update**\n- A later user message can change the governing interpretation, the target quantity, or the structure of the task; re-check the whole spec when that happens.\n- Judge the assistant against the resolved end-state, not against the partial state that existed before the latest disclosure.\n- If a later shard supplies the missing relation or modifier, prior \u201cneed more info\u201d behavior may become stale and should be flagged.\n- When the user has already provided enough information cumulatively, repeated clarification requests are a divergence, not caution.\n\n**3) Distinguish partial correctness from global correctness**\n- A response can be locally right for a subproblem yet still wrong if it does not carry through to the full asked-for output.\n- Separate correct intermediate derivations from the final task completion; do not let a valid subtotal mask an incomplete conclusion.\n- Prefer correction over endorsement when the assistant stops at an intermediate invariant, partial aggregate, or one branch of a multi-branch task.\n- Check whether the assistant solved the same overall problem the user asked, or quietly shifted to a smaller subquestion.\n\n**4) Watch for stale uncertainty, anchoring, and context fragmentation**\n- If the user has clarified an ambiguity, the analyzer should suppress discarded branches and judge only the supported interpretation.\n- Repeatedly optimizing around an old ambiguity after it has been resolved is anchoring on obsolete context.\n- A strong failure pattern is treating each shard as standalone instead of merging them into a single running model.\n- Compact away provisional assumptions and speculative branches once the user trajectory has made them unnecessary.\n\n**5) Detect hidden assumptions and unsupported pattern invention**\n- If the assistant introduces a rule, continuation, or default that the user never stated, flag it even when it seems plausible.\n- Do not let the analyzer bless an invented pattern just because earlier fragments made it look reasonable.\n- When the user later supplies concrete facts, those facts should override any earlier inferred continuation.\n- The key diagnostic is whether the assistant\u2019s reasoning is licensed by the full user trajectory, not whether it happened to land on the right answer.\n\n**6) Recompute aggregates when later turns change scope**\n- Look for late-disclosed quantities, repetition markers, grouping language, \u201cremaining/of the rest\u201d constraints, or per-entity modifiers that change the aggregation basis.\n- Verify that each user-provided component is included exactly once and at the correct scope.\n- Check for both undercounting and overcounting when the conversation accumulates entities, terms, or percentages over time.\n- When a later shard changes the target total or residual pool, earlier exact answers may become superseded.\n\n**7) Separate whole-vs-subset reasoning carefully**\n- Distinguish percentages or proportions of the whole from percentages of a remaining subset or other narrowed pool.\n- In any staged composition, confirm that intermediate quantities are chained against the correct reference set after each update.\n- A common divergence is carrying forward an old denominator or scope after the user has narrowed the base.\n- Recompute the current reference pool before judging the next derived quantity.\n\n**8) Verify output requirements, not just content**\n- Check whether the response matches explicit user constraints on format, brevity, completeness, or presentation.\n- A numerically or logically correct answer can still diverge if it adds explanation when the user asked for minimal output, or if it misses a required final form.\n- Treat \u201canswer the final thing\u201d as part of the task spec, not an optional style preference.\n\n**9) Make the corrective note concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The issue should name the missed action: recompute with the latest constraint, stop asking for already provided information, carry a subtotal through to the final result, or drop an unsupported branch.\n- Avoid vague advice like \u201cbe more careful\u201d; identify exactly where the assistant failed to update its model or finish the task.\n- If the main problem is stale control flow rather than arithmetic, say so directly.\n\n**10) Use a full-trajectory ledger to evaluate alignment**\n- Maintain a running constraint ledger from user turns and compare every assistant response against the current ledger.\n- First reconstruct the complete user specification, then test the assistant\u2019s current approach against that unified view.\n- This helps expose when the assistant is answering the latest fragment in isolation instead of the accumulated conversation state.\n- Preserve validated facts and correct deductions, but remove speculative scaffolding that could bias the next judgment."
